{-
   `@caller` parameters - Design-Test.md §11.1's F2.

   A parameter marked `@caller` is filled by the compiler, at every call that leaves it out, with a
   constant about the *call site*. Two fills: the call's location, and the source text of the
   expression that reached some other parameter. Between them they are what an assertion needs and no
   library function can reach - `check(a == b)` sees one `Bool`, and nothing in the library can see
   where it was written or what the author typed.

   Prior art, merged: C#'s `CallerArgumentExpression` is the text and Rust's `#[track_caller]` is the
   location. Neither language has both in one mechanism, and neither needs a macro system for it -
   which is the argument for this being four lines of the compiler rather than forty of a preprocessor.

   **The location record is matched by field name rather than by identity.** The compiler knows
   nothing about any particular `Site`: it lives in a library and not in `Core`, so what a `@caller`
   position asks for is a record whose fields are drawn from a fixed vocabulary - `file` and
   `function` are text, `line` and `column` are numbers - and a declaration takes the subset it wants.
   `Where` below is the one-field case, and it exists to say that the subset is real.

   Asserted by arithmetic on the filled values rather than by comparing text, because the goldens
   beside this file already pin the constants and a `String ==` would pass with both sides empty.
-}

data Site {file: String, line: Int, column: Int, function: String}
data Where {line: Int}

-- The location, whole. `caller$line` is what this file's own line numbering is asserted against, and
-- moving a call below changes the answer - which is the property being tested.
fn lineOf(@caller at: Site) -> Int = at.line
fn columnOf(@caller at: Site) -> Int = at.column
fn functionIs(name: String, @caller at: Site) -> Bool = at.function == name

-- A subset of the vocabulary. Nothing requires a declaration to ask for all four.
fn shortLine(@caller w: Where) -> Int = w.line

-- The text of what reached another parameter, which is the half a library cannot fake.
fn textOf(held: Bool, @caller(source: held) text: String) -> Int = truncate(length(text)) :: Int

{-
   A helper that passes its own `Site` on, which is the rule the whole feature rests on.

   A `@caller` parameter may be passed explicitly, by name, and is filled only when omitted - so this
   reports the line that called *it* rather than the line inside its own body. Without that, every
   assertion helper anyone writes reports its own body's location, which is the thing
   `#[track_caller]` exists to fix in Rust and which falls out here because "a parameter no argument
   reached is filled by its default" is already how defaults work.
-}
fn viaHelper(@caller at: Site) -> Int = lineOf(at: at)

-- And the same for the text fill: what reached `held` *here* is the helper's own expression, which is
-- the honest answer - the helper is what called the assertion.
fn textViaHelper(n: Int) -> Int = textOf(n > 0)

{-
   `@caller` is legal on any function, not only on a framework's - including a generic one, where the
   fill happens at the call site before any specialization exists.
-}
fn (Show(a)) describedAt(value: a, @caller at: Site) -> String = "{value}@{at.line}"

fn main() -> Int:
    let &total = 0 :: Int

    -- The line of the call and not of the declaration. These four are on consecutive lines, so the
    -- differences are what say the fill is per call rather than per callee.
    let first = lineOf()
    let second = lineOf()
    if second - first != 1 then return 1

    -- Two calls on one line, at different columns. The left one is written first, so its column is
    -- the smaller and the difference is negative - which is what says the fill is per *call* rather
    -- than per line.
    if columnOf() - columnOf() >= 0 then return 2

    if !functionIs("main") then return 3
    if shortLine() != lineOf() then return 4

    -- The text of the expression, which is `1 == 2` - six units, spanning the whole operand and not
    -- just the left one. An infix call is *emitted* at its left operand's location, so asking the
    -- resolved value where it came from would answer `1`; what is recorded is the span the author
    -- wrote. See ResolvedArg::written.
    if textOf(1 == 2) != 6 then return 5

    -- Nothing reached the position, so the text is empty rather than a guess.
    if textOf(True, text: "") != 0 then return 6

    -- The helper reports this line, not the one inside its body.
    if viaHelper() != lineOf() then return 7

    -- And the text fill inside a helper is the helper's own expression: `n > 0`.
    if textViaHelper(1) != 5 then return 8

    {-
       The generic case, asserted without naming a line: the text is `42@` and then whatever line
       this is, so what is checked is that a line got in there at all and that the two halves are in
       the right order. Comparing against a literal would make this fixture's answer depend on where
       in the file it sits, which is a property of the fixture rather than of the feature.
    -}
    let described = describedAt(42)
    if (truncate(length(described)) :: Int) <= 3 then return 9
    if String.unit(described, 0) != 52 then return 10        -- `4`
    if String.unit(described, 2) != 64 then return 11        -- `@`

    return total
